<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Dejima</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Dejima was a small artificial island in the harbour of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nagasaki">Nagasaki</a>. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-shogunate">Tokugawa Shogunate</a>'s policy of restricted foreign contact, it was the sole place where the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> were permitted to trade with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>. It became Japan's main channel for European goods and knowledge, the basis of 'Dutch learning' (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rangaku">rangaku</a>).</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dejima.jpg" alt="Dejima" width="320" height="263" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Isaac Titsingh · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Plattegrond_van_Deshima.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…1630s, banned Japanese from travelling abroad, expelled the Portuguese and confined the Dutch from 1641 to the artificial island of <b>Dejima</b> in Nagasaki, where Chinese merchants also traded; further contacts with Korea, the Ryukyu Kingdom and the Ainu were channelled through the domains of Tsushima, Satsuma and Matsumae.</blockquote><a href="/empire/tokugawa-shogunate">Tokugawa Shogunate · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Since 1633 the policy of sakoku had kept Japan closed, with only the Dutch trading at <b>Dejima</b>.</blockquote><a href="/movement/meiji-restoration">Meiji Restoration · Background</a></li></ul></section>
